do $do$
declare p jsonb := $pl$[{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-119","t":"Proteins","s":"A protein consists of a single polypeptide chain. In a second version of the protein, one amino acid with a hydrophobic R group is replaced by an amino acid with a charged R group. The second version folds differently and functions less effectively. Which explanation best connects the amino acid replacement to these observations?","a":"D","c":[["A","The two versions have identical primary structures because they contain the same number of amino acids, so sequence cannot explain the functional difference.",false,"This is the same-length-means-same-sequence trap. Primary structure is the amino acid sequence, so replacing one amino acid changes primary structure even when chain length stays constant. Fix: Compare amino acid identities and order, not just chain length."],["B","Only peptide bonds determine the protein’s folded shape, so changing an R group cannot explain the functional difference.",false,"This is the peptide-bonds-only trap. Peptide bonds link amino acids, but interactions involving R groups also help determine the folded shape and therefore protein function. Fix: Trace how the changed R group could alter interactions within the protein."],["C","The replacement changes quaternary structure because quaternary structure describes how a single polypeptide folds into its functional shape.",false,"This is the tertiary–quaternary confusion trap. Quaternary structure involves interactions between multiple polypeptides; the overall three-dimensional fold of this single chain is its tertiary structure. Fix: Count the polypeptide chains before identifying tertiary or quaternary structure."],["D","The replacement changes R-group interactions, which can alter the protein’s three-dimensional shape and function.",true,"R-group interactions help determine a protein’s tertiary structure. Changing an R group can change these interactions and the resulting fold, affecting the protein’s ability to function."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-119-v1","t":"Proteins","s":"Scientists compare single-chain oxygen-binding proteins from two fish species. The proteins contain the same number and types of amino acids, but some amino acids occur in different positions. The proteins fold differently, and one binds oxygen less effectively. Which explanation best accounts for this difference in function?","a":"C","c":[["A","The proteins have identical primary structures because their chains are equally long, so primary structure cannot explain the binding difference.",false,"Same-length-means-same-sequence trap: Equal amino acid counts do not establish identical primary structures. Moving amino acids to different positions changes the sequence even when composition and length remain unchanged. Fix: Compare the amino acid order rather than only counting amino acids."],["B","Peptide bonds alone control protein folding, so differences in R-group positions cannot account for the binding difference.",false,"Peptide-bonds-only trap: This overlooks interactions involving R groups that help establish a protein’s folded shape. Peptide bonds connect the chain, but they are not the only contributors to its structure. Fix: Identify how different R-group positions could change interactions during folding."],["C","The different amino acid orders can change R-group interactions, altering the folded shape and the ability to bind oxygen.",true,"Amino acid order influences which R groups interact as a protein folds. Different interactions can produce a different three-dimensional shape, affecting how effectively the protein binds oxygen."],["D","The proteins differ in quaternary structure because that level describes the three-dimensional shape of each individual chain.",false,"Tertiary–quaternary confusion trap: An individual polypeptide’s overall fold is its tertiary structure. Quaternary structure describes the association of multiple polypeptide chains, not either single-chain protein described here. Fix: Check how many polypeptide chains are involved before naming the structural level."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-119-v2","t":"Proteins","s":"A laboratory designs two proteins for a coating that holds a dye. Each protein is a single polypeptide containing 60 amino acids. Their sequences differ at four positions, where the amino acids have different R-group properties. Tests reveal different three-dimensional shapes and weaker dye binding by one protein. Which explanation best connects the sequence differences to the binding results?","a":"D","c":[["A","Equal chain lengths establish identical primary structures, so amino acid sequences cannot account for the unequal dye binding.",false,"The same-length-means-same-sequence trap confuses amino acid count with amino acid sequence. These proteins have different primary structures because their amino acid identities differ at four positions. Fix: Compare the amino acids at corresponding positions rather than counting them."],["B","Peptide bonds alone control each protein’s folded shape, so differences in R groups cannot account for the unequal dye binding.",false,"The peptide-bonds-only trap overlooks interactions that shape a protein beyond linking its amino acids. R-group interactions help establish the folded shape needed for effective binding. Fix: Identify how different R-group properties could change interactions during folding."],["C","The different folds indicate different quaternary structures because quaternary structure describes the overall shape of an individual polypeptide.",false,"The tertiary–quaternary confusion trap assigns a single chain’s overall fold to the wrong structural level. Tertiary structure describes that fold; quaternary structure involves multiple polypeptide chains interacting. Fix: Determine how many polypeptide chains are involved before naming the structural level."],["D","Differences in R-group interactions can produce different folds, changing how effectively each protein binds the dye.",true,"The amino acid sequence determines which R groups can interact as a protein folds. Different interactions can alter the protein’s three-dimensional shape and therefore its ability to bind the dye."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-119-v3","t":"Proteins","s":"A bacterial protein made of one polypeptide helps cells attach to surfaces. Researchers test two forms, each containing 80 amino acids. The forms differ in the order of amino acids within one short section. They have different folded shapes, and one supports weaker attachment. What best explains these results?","a":"C","c":[["A","Both forms have the same primary structure because each contains 80 amino acids, so their sequences cannot account for weaker attachment.",false,"The same-length-means-same-sequence trap confuses chain length with primary structure. Different amino acid orders constitute different primary structures, even when both chains contain 80 amino acids. Fix: Compare the order of amino acids rather than the total number."],["B","Peptide bonds alone establish the folded shape, so rearranging amino acids cannot account for weaker attachment.",false,"The peptide-bonds-only trap overlooks interactions that stabilize protein folding. Peptide bonds connect amino acids, while R-group interactions also influence the shape needed for attachment. Fix: Identify how repositioned R groups could change interactions and folding."],["C","The different amino acid order can alter R-group interactions, changing the folded shape and its ability to support attachment.",true,"Amino acid order determines where R groups occur along the chain. Their interactions help shape the protein, so a different sequence can produce a fold that supports attachment less effectively."],["D","The altered fold represents a change in quaternary structure because that level describes the shape of one polypeptide.",false,"The tertiary–quaternary confusion trap assigns a single chain’s overall fold to the wrong structural level. Tertiary structure describes that fold; quaternary structure concerns associations among multiple polypeptides. Fix: Determine how many polypeptides are involved before naming the structural level."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-120","t":"Proteins","s":"Researchers compare two versions of a protein that binds a target molecule. Each version consists of one intact polypeptide containing 100 amino acids. The versions differ only in the amino acid at position 40. Measurements are made under identical conditions.\n\nProtein version | R group at position 40 | Folded shape | Protein molecules bound to target (%)\nOriginal | Hydrophobic | Reference shape | 90\nModified | Charged | Different from reference | 20\n\nWhich statement best describes the relationship in the data and connects it to protein structure and function?","a":"D","c":[["A","The versions have identical primary structures because they contain the same number of amino acids, so the binding difference cannot reflect a sequence change.",false,"Length–sequence confusion: Primary structure is the amino acid sequence, not merely the number of amino acids. The replacement at position 40 changes that sequence despite equal chain lengths. Fix: Compare amino acid identities and order, not just chain length."],["B","The modified protein binds fewer target molecules because replacing the R group breaks peptide bonds and shortens the polypeptide.",false,"Side-chain–backbone confusion: R groups influence folding interactions, whereas peptide bonds connect amino acids in the backbone. Both measured proteins have intact, equally long polypeptides, so chain shortening does not explain the binding decrease. Fix: Separate changes in R-group interactions from breaks in the peptide backbone."],["C","The modified protein’s different folded shape represents a change in quaternary structure that explains its lower target binding.",false,"Tertiary–quaternary confusion: Quaternary structure involves interactions between multiple polypeptides. Each version contains only one polypeptide, so its overall three-dimensional folding concerns tertiary structure. Fix: Count polypeptide chains before identifying tertiary or quaternary structure."],["D","The modified protein has a different folded shape and lower target binding, consistent with changed R-group interactions affecting protein function.",true,"Target binding decreases from 90% to 20% as folded shape changes. Replacing a hydrophobic R group with a charged R group can alter interactions within the protein, connecting the sequence change to altered folding and binding function."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-120-v1","t":"Proteins","s":"A laboratory evaluates two forms of a structural protein used as an elastic spring. Each molecule is a single, unbroken polypeptide of 150 amino acids. Only the amino acid at position 65 differs between the forms. Under the same testing conditions, researchers measure folded shape and the greatest extension from which each molecule returns to its starting shape.\n\nProtein form | R group at position 65 | Folded shape | Greatest reversible extension (nm)\nInitial | Uncharged and hydrophilic | Compact | 4\nAltered | Hydrophobic | Less compact | 9\n\nWhich conclusion best connects the observed pattern to protein structure and function?","a":"A","c":[["A","The altered form has a less compact fold and greater reversible extension, consistent with changed R-group interactions affecting its elasticity.",true,"Reversible extension increases from 4 nm to 9 nm alongside a change in folded shape. The amino acid replacement can change R-group interactions, linking a sequence difference to altered folding and the protein’s elastic function."],["B","The forms have the same primary structure because both contain 150 amino acids, so their different elasticity cannot be associated with a sequence difference.",false,"Length–sequence confusion: Equal numbers of amino acids do not establish identical primary structures. The different amino acid at position 65 changes the sequence even though chain length stays constant. Fix: Check amino acid identities and their order when comparing primary structures."],["C","The altered form stretches farther because the R-group replacement breaks peptide bonds, producing a shorter polypeptide.",false,"Side-chain–backbone confusion: Peptide bonds join amino acids, while R groups participate in interactions that influence folding. The data describe unbroken chains of equal length, not shortened chains. Fix: Distinguish altered side-chain interactions from damage to the polypeptide backbone."],["D","The less compact fold is a change in quaternary structure that accounts for the altered form’s greater reversible extension.",false,"Tertiary–quaternary confusion: Quaternary structure describes associations among multiple polypeptides. The overall fold of either single-chain protein is its tertiary structure. Fix: Count the polypeptides in each protein before assigning its structural level."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-120-v2","t":"Proteins","s":"A materials laboratory tests two forms of a structural protein that helps a soft gel withstand compression. Every protein molecule contains a single, unbroken polypeptide of 180 amino acids. The forms differ only at amino acid position 72. Gels containing equal amounts of either form are tested under the same conditions.\n\nProtein form | R group at position 72 | Overall folded shape | Maximum force supported before gel collapses (N)\nStandard | Charged | Compact | 8\nReplacement | Uncharged and hydrophilic | Less compact | 3\n\nWhich interpretation connects the measured relationship to protein structure and function?","a":"B","c":[["A","The forms have the same primary structure because both contain 180 amino acids, so their different performance cannot involve an amino acid sequence difference.",false,"Length–sequence confusion: Equal numbers of amino acids do not establish identical sequences. The different amino acid at position 72 changes primary structure even though chain length stays constant. Fix: Check amino acid identity and order when comparing primary structures."],["B","The replacement form is less compact and supports less force, consistent with altered R-group interactions changing folding and structural support.",true,"The supported force drops from 8 N to 3 N alongside a change in folded shape. Changing an amino acid’s R group can alter folding interactions, linking the sequence difference to a shape change and reduced structural support."],["C","The replacement form supports less force because the R-group change breaks backbone peptide bonds, producing a shorter protein.",false,"Side-chain–backbone confusion: A difference in R groups is not a break in the peptide backbone. The stem specifies unbroken chains of equal length, ruling out shortening as the explanation. Fix: Distinguish altered side-chain interactions from broken bonds connecting amino acids."],["D","The replacement form supports less force because its less compact folding indicates a change in quaternary structure.",false,"Tertiary–quaternary confusion: Overall folding within one polypeptide concerns tertiary structure. Quaternary structure describes associations between multiple polypeptides, whereas each protein molecule here contains only one. Fix: Identify how many polypeptides form each protein before naming its structural level."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-120-v3","t":"Proteins","s":"A team studies two forms of an antifreeze protein from an Arctic fish. Each molecule is a single, unbroken polypeptide of 120 amino acids, and the forms differ only in the amino acid at position 28. Both forms are tested at the same temperature, pH, and concentration.\n\nProtein form | R group at position 28 | Folded shape | Reduction in ice crystal growth (%)\nNative | Uncharged and hydrophilic | Reference shape | 85\nVariant | Hydrophobic | Altered from reference | 15\n\nWhich statement best describes the pattern in the data and links it to protein structure and function?","a":"D","c":[["A","Both forms have the same primary structure because each contains 120 amino acids, so the drop in activity cannot come from a sequence change.",false,"Length–sequence confusion: primary structure is the order and identity of amino acids, not the chain length. Replacing the amino acid at position 28 changes the sequence even though both chains have 120 amino acids. Fix: Compare which amino acid sits at each position, not only how many there are."],["B","The variant reduces ice growth less because the new R group breaks peptide bonds, leaving a shorter polypeptide.",false,"Side-chain–backbone confusion: peptide bonds form the backbone, while R groups affect folding interactions. The stem states both forms are unbroken 120-amino-acid chains, so no shortening occurred. Fix: Check the stem for chain length before blaming backbone breaks for a functional change."],["C","The variant's altered folded shape reflects a change in quaternary structure, which explains its weaker ice-growth reduction.",false,"Tertiary–quaternary confusion: quaternary structure requires interactions among multiple polypeptides. Each form here is a single polypeptide, so its overall three-dimensional fold is tertiary structure. Fix: Count the polypeptide chains before naming the structural level involved."],["D","The variant has an altered folded shape and much weaker ice-growth reduction, consistent with a changed R group altering folding interactions and thus function.",true,"Ice-growth reduction drops from 85% to 15% when the folded shape changes. Swapping a hydrophilic R group for a hydrophobic one can change interactions that guide folding, so the sequence change alters shape, which alters the protein's function."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-121","t":"Proteins","s":"A protein consists of a single polypeptide and binds a small molecule in a pocket formed by its folded structure. A hydrophobic amino acid buried near the pocket is replaced with an amino acid that has a charged R group. The chain length and peptide-bond connections remain unchanged. Which prediction best connects this replacement to protein function?","a":"C","c":[["A","Intact peptide-bond connections preserve the primary structure, so the pocket’s shape and binding ability must remain unchanged.",false,"Bond-integrity trap: Primary structure is the amino acid sequence, not merely the connections holding the chain together. Replacing an amino acid changes that sequence even when chain length and peptide-bond connections are preserved. Fix: Compare amino acid identities, not just chain length or bond connections."],["B","The replacement changes secondary structure because secondary structure is defined by R-group interactions, reducing binding to the small molecule.",false,"Secondary–tertiary confusion: Secondary structure is stabilized by hydrogen bonds between backbone atoms, not defined by R-group interactions. Changes in R-group interactions can alter tertiary folding and therefore the binding pocket. Fix: Identify whether the described interactions involve backbone atoms or R groups."],["C","Altered R-group interactions may change tertiary structure and the pocket’s shape, reducing binding to the small molecule.",true,"Replacing a hydrophobic R group with a charged R group can change interactions within the protein. These changes can alter its three-dimensional folding and the binding pocket’s shape, reducing its ability to bind the small molecule."],["D","The replacement disrupts quaternary structure by separating the protein’s polypeptide subunits, reducing binding to the small molecule.",false,"Quaternary-structure trap: Quaternary structure involves interactions between multiple polypeptides, but this protein contains only one. Its binding pocket can change through altered tertiary structure without any subunits separating. Fix: Count the polypeptide chains before attributing a change to quaternary structure."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-121-v1","t":"Proteins","s":"A single-chain protein used in an elastic material returns to its original shape after gentle stretching. An ionic interaction between two R groups helps stabilize its folded form. Researchers produce a version in which one of these charged amino acids is replaced by an amino acid with a nonpolar R group; chain length and peptide-bond connectivity are unchanged. Which prediction best explains how this replacement could affect the protein’s shape recovery?","a":"C","c":[["A","Unchanged peptide-bond connectivity preserves primary structure, so the protein must retain its original shape recovery.",false,"Bond-integrity trap: Keeping peptide-bond connectivity unchanged does not preserve the amino acid sequence when an amino acid is replaced. The altered sequence can change folding and function. Fix: Compare the original and replacement amino acid identities before judging whether primary structure changed."],["B","The replacement directly changes secondary structure, which is defined by ionic interactions between R groups, impairing shape recovery.",false,"Secondary–tertiary confusion: Secondary structure involves hydrogen bonding between backbone atoms, not ionic interactions between R groups. The disrupted R-group interaction instead contributes to tertiary folding. Fix: Locate each interaction on the backbone or R groups before assigning a structural level."],["C","Loss of the ionic interaction may alter tertiary folding, making the protein less able to recover its original shape.",true,"The nonpolar R group cannot maintain the original ionic interaction with the remaining charged R group. Losing this stabilizing interaction may change the protein’s three-dimensional shape and impair the shape recovery required for its mechanical function."],["D","The replacement disrupts quaternary structure by separating polypeptide subunits, preventing the protein from recovering its original shape.",false,"Quaternary-structure trap: A protein with only one polypeptide has no quaternary structure or separate polypeptide subunits to pull apart. Its shape recovery can change through disruption of tertiary structure. Fix: Count the polypeptides before proposing that subunit separation causes the functional change."]]}]$pl$::jsonb; r jsonb; c jsonb; v_ci uuid; v_civ uuid; n int;
begin
  if jsonb_array_length(p) <> 10 then raise exception 'chunk size'; end if;
  if exists (select 1 from app.content_items ci where ci.exam_pack_version_id='2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7' and ci.content_key in (select x->>'k' from jsonb_array_elements(p) x))
    then raise exception 'chunk already loaded'; end if;
  for r in select * from jsonb_array_elements(p) loop
    insert into app.content_items (exam_pack_version_id, content_key, item_type, title, status)
    values ('2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7', r->>'k', 'mcq', r->>'t', 'draft') returning id into v_ci;
    insert into app.content_item_versions (content_item_id, version_num, stem, stimulus, content_hash, status, review_status, canonical_answer_1)
    values (v_ci, 1, r->>'s', null, md5(r->>'k'), 'draft', 'tutor_review_pending', r->>'a') returning id into v_civ;
    for c in select * from jsonb_array_elements(r->'c') loop
      insert into app.mcq_choices (content_item_version_id, choice_key, choice_text, is_correct, rationale)
      values (v_civ, c->>0, c->>1, (c->>2)::boolean, c->>3);
    end loop;
  end loop;
  select count(*) into n from app.content_items ci join app.content_item_versions v on v.content_item_id=ci.id join app.mcq_choices m on m.content_item_version_id=v.id
   where ci.exam_pack_version_id='2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7' and ci.status='draft' and ci.content_key in (select x->>'k' from jsonb_array_elements(p) x);
  if n <> 40 then raise exception 'chunk 8: expected 40 choice rows, got %', n; end if;
  raise notice 'chunk 8: loaded 10 draft items';
end
$do$;
